WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.459
And when, you know, it's
a performance-based industry.

00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:04.838
So when we perform, we're hyped. Like, it's
a lot more than just that play, right there.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.548
There's a lot that goes into it.

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:07.716
And I don't think
a lotta people can relate.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:10.844
That's why they're so quick to penalize
or make it a bad thing

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:11.720
when it's, like, really like,

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.180
I'm showing you that I really care
about what I do.

00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:17.767
- I have a crazy story
 about, not necessarily this studio,

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:21.688
but just, like, right underneath us
in this parking lot.
- Mmhmm. Tell me!

00:00:22.564 --> 00:00:24.899
- So last time I parked in this parking lot,

00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:27.444
there's a guy who's, like, pretty
much like one of my OGs out here now.

00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:29.988
His name's Paul. Shout-out to Paul.

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:33.575
He lived in the underground tunnels,
like, homeless

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:36.995
underneath Vegas for, I think he said
three years.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.123
And he goes down there
now. He has a foundation called Shine a Light.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:42.000
And he goes and gets people out.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.795
And I went down with him one weekend,
and we parked in this parking lot and went,

00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:47.005
like, underneath the bridge right here.

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:49.174
And we're underneath the sewer
tunnels, walking around,

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:52.260
giving supplies to, like, homeless people
that live down there.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.263
So I'm like—when you sent me this, I was like,
"This seems really weird."
- "I've been here."

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:57.474
Yeah. OK, first thing, that's amazing.

00:00:57.474 --> 00:00:59.309
But I have a bunch of questions.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:01.352
- All right.
- First off, did not know

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:04.522
there were underground tunnels in Vegas
where a lot of homeless people live.

00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:06.649
- Yep.
- Did you know that before you came here?

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:09.694
- No, I did not.
- OK, so talk to me about that.

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:12.989
- I mean, like everybody else, I thought
only the Strip existed in Vegas.

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:16.868
- Yeah.
- So, meeting this guy Paul, he's
been really helpful as far as, like,

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:18.161
my recovery journey.
- Yeah.

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:22.457
- And I went down there with him,
and there's, like, 1,500

00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:23.958
people living down there.

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:26.461
And we brought them supplies like batteries

00:01:26.461 --> 00:01:29.172
and socks and snacks and water
and stuff like that.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:32.175
- That's amazing.
- And there's people down there living.

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:34.803
And we came across this one guy, they
were trying to get him to come out.

00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:37.806
But he was like, "Nah, I'm comfortable."
Like, "I'm not leaving here."

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:39.349
So he was just, like, comfortable
living down there.

00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:42.018
And they have just their own ways,
and they don't have to interact

00:01:42.018 --> 00:01:43.937
with people. You know, they probably been hurt

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:46.564
in some way by the world
that they wanna live in that place,

00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:48.274
and they feel like
that's a good place for them, so—

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:51.569
- Yeah.
- It was an incredible—just from my perspective.

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:52.779
- Yeah. That's so interesting

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.949
to think, like, their comfort is coming from
this thing that we probably, like,

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:56.741
can't even fathom.

00:01:56.741 --> 00:02:00.411
They're like, "Well, this is kind of
my reality now, and I'm OK in this moment."

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:04.958
So, Darren Waller, NFL
tight end, goes down in these tunnels.

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:07.001
Were there any reactions from people like,

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:09.254
"Is that him?" Or were
most people unaware?

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:10.672
- Nah, they didn't really know, cuz

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:11.464
you know, they were just kinda like,

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.550
"What y'all doin'? What y'all want?"
- Yeah.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:13.883
so it's a, it's a crazy environment
down there.
- Yeah.

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:15.718
It's like its own community.

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:19.055
- Right.
- Oh, my gosh. Well, that's amazing
you went down there and helped.

00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:21.099
Have you only gone once?
Are you gonna go more?

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:23.184
- I've only been once.
- OK.
- Probably when the

00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:24.769
offseason comes around
I'll go down there again,

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:26.980
you know?
- Yeah.
- It's, it's just incredible.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.232
I wanna, probably wanna bring some of my
teammates or somebody with me, you know? It's dope.

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:31.151
- I love that. OK,
so I have one more question about it

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:32.402
cuz this is so interesting to me.

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:35.321
So this guy you're saying is your OG,
how did you meet him?

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.115
- So I got connected with him through—

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:40.952
there's a Ford dealership
that I have a partnership with out here.
- Yeah.

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:45.331
- And they do a lot of charity work, and
they work with this guy and his foundation.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.877
So I met Paul, and then I was like,
"Paul's, like, a real dude."

00:02:48.877 --> 00:02:51.296
And he invited me to, like, some meetings
out here and just hanging around,

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:54.507
you know, like-minded people.
- Yeah.
- And he's really been, become somebody that

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:55.842
I lean on.
- Yeah.

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.927
Oh, my gosh. I love this.
You have just become,

00:02:57.927 --> 00:03:02.307
I feel like, such an important figure
in this community for Vegas.

00:03:02.307 --> 00:03:05.935
Every time I've told somebody I'm coming
to your interview, they get incredibly excited.

00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:08.688
There's so much to dive into.
I wanna talk about all the stuff

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:11.858
you've done with the community,
your journey and all these things.

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:14.444
But, you know, there's something
I have to get out of the way.
- All right.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:18.448
- Because everybody said, "This needs to be
the first thing you ask him about."

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:20.783
- Oh, boy.
- This taunting call. [Laughing]

00:03:23.161 --> 00:03:27.290
- Oh, boy.
- How do we feel about this taunting rule,

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:29.751
and will you ever spike a football again?

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.630
- The taunting rule,
I feel like, is a little over the top, you know?

00:03:33.671 --> 00:03:37.425
- Yeah.
- I get why they may have thrown
a flag on mine because mine was, like,

00:03:37.425 --> 00:03:39.260
on the opponent's sideline.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.013
So the ref, like, usually
it's like, if you go on the sideline

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:44.182
and you do something,
like, they're instantly gonna throw it.
- Yeah.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:47.268
- So I was just, you know, hyped
because we had been struggling to that point.

00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:49.479
That was our first first down of the game,
and it was almost halftime.

00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:53.399
So I was just like, "Let's go." Like,
I was just, like, getting some frustration out.

00:03:53.816 --> 00:03:54.400
And they threw that.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.404
And I was just like, "Man, that's nuts."
And then I spiked one, like, later

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:01.574
in the game. Like, it wasn't as egregious,
and it was, like, in the middle of the field.

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:03.785
And they came up to me, and
they were like, "Whoa, you gotta

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:06.120
chill out. You gotta chill out." I'm like, nah.
- What?!

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.706
"I can't be passionate
and have fun and be exciting?"

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:12.543
That's my issue with the taunting rule is
I feel like fans

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:15.630
like seeing their favorite players
get into it in that way—
- Right.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.425
- And, like, feel aggressive and be pumped
when you do something that's like a big,

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:23.012
you know, momentum swing in the game.
Like, do you almost feel like you're

00:04:23.012 --> 00:04:26.641
having to hold back your emotions a lot
cuz that flag is gonna come?

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.644
- I mean, I don't think I'm really gonna
focus too much on that.
- Yeah.

00:04:29.644 --> 00:04:32.188
- Maybe, like, not throw the ball crazy
all over the place.

00:04:32.188 --> 00:04:34.399
But I can still, like,
just, like, drop the ball lightly

00:04:34.482 --> 00:04:36.484
and still get up and kinda
go crazy

00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:39.862
cuz I don't feel like a lot of people
can relate to that level of emotion

00:04:39.862 --> 00:04:43.157
cuz we put so much into the game
on a daily basis.

00:04:43.157 --> 00:04:43.908
And when, you know, it's
a performance-based industry.

00:04:43.908 --> 00:04:45.368
And when, you know, it's
a performance-based industry.

00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:48.746
So when we perform, we're hyped. Like, it's
a lot more than just that play, right there.

00:04:48.746 --> 00:04:50.456
There's a lot that goes into it.

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.624
And I don't think
a lotta people can relate.

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.752
That's why they're so quick to penalize
or make it a bad thing

00:04:54.752 --> 00:04:55.628
when it's, like, really like,

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:58.089
I'm showing you that I really care
about what I do.

00:04:58.131 --> 00:05:00.383
- Yeah. Like, I'm trying to win this.

00:05:00.508 --> 00:05:02.010
- Yeah.
- No, I love it. OK,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.846
tell me, what was your
"welcome to the NFL" moment

00:05:04.846 --> 00:05:06.681
where you were like,
"All right, this is different. Like,

00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:07.932
these are the big boys"?

00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:10.351
- It was my rookie year in Baltimore, 2015.

00:05:10.351 --> 00:05:13.438
We were playing the Cleveland Browns,
and there was a safety on their team named

00:05:13.438 --> 00:05:17.608
Donte Whitner. He played for the 49ers,
was where he was most well-known.

00:05:17.608 --> 00:05:21.404
And he caught me over the middle
on a crossing route, hit me here in my chest

00:05:21.404 --> 00:05:22.322
and gave me a concussion.

00:05:23.948 --> 00:05:26.159
- [Laughing] You're like, "Oh, boy.

00:05:26.451 --> 00:05:30.830
Is this gonna be week in, week out?"
- Just off the whiplash and like,
 I, like, crawled probably like five yards.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:31.456
And I was like, "Nah,

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:34.625
I gotta get up." Like, I just ran off the field
and then went straight to the tunnel.

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:35.501
My day was over with, so.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.588
- Yeah, you're like, "Get me in this booth, 'Te.
Like, this is not meant for me."

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:39.339
- Donte Whitner.

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:40.673
- Yeah, I love that.

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:43.843
OK, so, my mom was very excited about me
doing this interview

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:46.846
because you are her fantasy
football tight end.

00:05:46.929 --> 00:05:49.015
-  OK.
 - Now, are you one of those players,

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:52.143
it's like, "I do not care about fantasy."
Do you like fantasy?

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:54.896
Did you ever play fantasy?
What's your stance on it?

00:05:55.438 --> 00:05:58.483
- I think I played fantasy twice in my life.

00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:02.445
One time I think I was in middle school.
- Yeah.
- And that was the year Tom Brady

00:06:02.445 --> 00:06:04.697
blew his knee out
the first game of the season.

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:06.616
And so I was like,
"Yeah, I'm not doing this."

00:06:06.616 --> 00:06:09.118
- Did you have Brady?
- Yeah.
- Oh, yeah.
- [Laughing]
- Then you were done.

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:11.954
- Yeah.
- Yeah. Fantasy is, like, 75 percent luck.

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:14.123
It drives me crazy, but
- Right.
- I cannot stop playing it.

00:06:14.207 --> 00:06:17.877
- And then I was out of the league
in 2017, and a girl I was

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.838
dating at the time, her family needed,
like, one more body for her fantasy league.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.049
- Yeah.
- And I was like, "Sure." I pick my team.

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:26.177
I thought they were good players,
but I think I was, like, second-to-last in the league.
- [Laughing]

00:06:26.761 --> 00:06:28.679
- So I really didn't know what I was doing.

00:06:28.679 --> 00:06:31.682
- I mean, that's kind of how it goes.
That's kind of how it goes.

00:06:31.933 --> 00:06:34.310
OK, so for people
who are very into fantasy,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.188
you are obviously one of the top players.
You get a lot of points.

00:06:37.688 --> 00:06:42.360
But tell me, like, five guys
you would be like, "OK, I'm OK being traded

00:06:42.652 --> 00:06:45.822
for these guys." Cuz there was a trade
controversy in my league for you.

00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:47.198
Someone didn't want to give you up,

00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:49.992
but someone really wanted
to give up a lot of pieces to get you. So

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:51.661
I'm just curious as to your stance. Like, who are some guys

00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:53.663
you'd be like, "OK,
I understand this trade"?

00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:57.375
- I'll probably say just all those
 those running backs, you know?
- Yeah.

00:06:57.375 --> 00:07:01.629
- Cuz they can rack up so many points
with so many touches, like McCaffrey,

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:05.508
Alvin Kamara, Dalvin Cook…
- Mmhmm.
- Saquon Barkley.

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:07.844
I mean, there's so many that can just
rack it up. Derrick Henry.
- Mmhmm.

00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:12.974
- So there's any of those running backs or,
you know, a wide receiver that's just getting

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:16.602
all kind of targets or even, like,
I mean, any quarterback, like a Kyler Murray.
- Totally. All the points.

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:19.188
- He's doing all that crazy stuff, so.
- Yeah.

00:07:19.188 --> 00:07:21.732
Well, one thing I really like about tight
ends, you know, in this day,

00:07:21.732 --> 00:07:23.860
is you guys really are receivers, right?

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.611
Like, you are guaranteed points.

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.363
You, Travis Kelce,

00:07:27.363 --> 00:07:30.575
George Kittle. Like, it's,
you get a lot of points from tight ends.

00:07:30.575 --> 00:07:33.870
How have you seen that position just evolve
throughout your playing career?

00:07:34.745 --> 00:07:37.790
- It's just guys that can do everything
that football requires, honestly.
- Mmhmm.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:42.462
- Because you look at it it's
we gotta block big defensive ends,

00:07:42.587 --> 00:07:45.756
Khalil Macks and Chase Youngs,
week in and week out, Myles Garretts.

00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:51.220
And then you gotta run routes against
defensive backs that are fast and talented,

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:54.390
and you gotta be, you know, quicker
and stronger than linebackers as well.

00:07:54.432 --> 00:07:57.643
So it's like
- Yeah.
- you gotta be able
to do all these skills

00:07:57.643 --> 00:07:59.145
that are already so hard in their own right.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:02.064
And you have to do what offensive linemen
do and do what wide receivers do.
- Mmhmm.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.525
- So they're asking a lot of you.

00:08:04.567 --> 00:08:09.071
And we've just kinda added an athletic
speed, explosiveness element to it.
- Mmhmm.

00:08:09.071 --> 00:08:11.365
- It's not just, like, a big, stocky,
burly position anymore.

00:08:11.407 --> 00:08:14.660
- Yeah. Like, you guys can do anything.
- Right.
- OK, so I'm gonna name some receivers.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.621
You tell me what makes them special,

00:08:16.621 --> 00:08:18.873
OK? What makes Travis Kelce special?

00:08:19.457 --> 00:08:20.708
- Overall control.

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:23.294
Like, nobody is ever dictating
the game to him.

00:08:23.628 --> 00:08:26.297
He's never running, like, 100
miles per hour.

00:08:26.506 --> 00:08:28.633
I mean, my coach always told me,
like, you don't have to run every route
in fifth gear,

00:08:28.633 --> 00:08:29.509
but he can run those routes in,

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:31.093
like, third or fourth gear, and just be smooth

00:08:31.093 --> 00:08:32.595
and just have you falling
all over the place.
- Yeah.

00:08:32.595 --> 00:08:33.763
And then turn it on.
- Yes.

00:08:33.763 --> 00:08:36.849
- Yeah, yeah.
- Just, his change-of-speed is ridiculous.

00:08:37.016 --> 00:08:39.727
- OK, what makes George Kittle special?

00:08:39.727 --> 00:08:43.314
- He's just an animal, you know?
Mindset-wise and blocking people.

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:45.066
He just loves dumping
people on their head.

00:08:45.066 --> 00:08:46.776
And just, he just plays with,

00:08:46.776 --> 00:08:48.611
like, people don't
wanna really get involved with that.

00:08:48.611 --> 00:08:52.740
He's so physical, and he'll just drag
five people
- Yeah.
- down the field.
- Easily.

00:08:52.782 --> 00:08:54.700
Yeah. OK, what about T.J. Hockenson?

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.620
Cuz that's somebody that I think
is really emerging right now.

00:08:57.912 --> 00:09:01.374
- I mean, similar to George Kittle, honestly.
They all come from that tight end factory,

00:09:01.374 --> 00:09:02.708
you know,
- Mmhmm.
- from Iowa.

00:09:02.708 --> 00:09:06.754
So...ability to block and then make
explosive plays in the passing game.

00:09:06.754 --> 00:09:07.964
He's faster than you think he is.
- Yeah.

00:09:07.964 --> 00:09:10.258
- He can jump higher than you think he can.

00:09:10.258 --> 00:09:12.301
And he's just real, real smooth.

00:09:12.343 --> 00:09:14.345
I noticed that at the Tight End University,

00:09:14.345 --> 00:09:16.514
like, he's, like, I was like,
wow, like, he's real smooth.

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:19.350
- Yeah, he's different. Yeah. OK,
what makes Darren Waller special?

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.437
- I'll say just a combination of speed,

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:29.193
route-running, versatility from out wide,
in the slot, hand in the ground,

00:09:29.777 --> 00:09:34.365
being able to pass-protect, run-block
in the run game
- Mmhmm.
- in big personnel groups.

00:09:34.365 --> 00:09:36.951
So there's nothing on the field
that my team doesn't ask me to do.

00:09:36.951 --> 00:09:39.495
So I feel I just have versatility.
- Yeah.

00:09:39.704 --> 00:09:42.498
I mean, it's no secret
you are incredibly talented, right?

00:09:42.498 --> 00:09:45.668
But if we say that a 10 is, like, peak,

00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:49.005
prime Darren Waller, OK?
Like, that's the ceiling is a 10.

00:09:49.338 --> 00:09:51.757
Where do you feel like
you are at this moment? What number?

00:09:53.175 --> 00:09:55.177
- Wow. Yeah, definitely not a 10.
I'll probably say, like,

00:09:56.178 --> 00:09:58.306
maybe like a 7, 7.5.

00:09:58.306 --> 00:09:59.515
- OK, so this isn't even full form.

00:09:59.515 --> 00:10:01.976
- I feel like there's
still a lot of growing to do.
- Yeah.

00:10:02.435 --> 00:10:04.645
What is that growing? What's in that gap?

00:10:04.854 --> 00:10:07.106
What will take you from a 7 to a 10?

00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:09.859
- You know, being able to take over games
week in and week out.

00:10:10.526 --> 00:10:13.779
You know, make those winning plays.
Cuz there was a lot of opportunities

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:18.868
where, you know, we lost games,
like, in the last minutes, in the last seconds.

00:10:18.868 --> 00:10:20.870
So it's like, you know, making those plays
from my teams, like,

00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:23.039
you know, maybe like a Kobe
Bryant would. Like,

00:10:23.039 --> 00:10:23.998
they would always lean on him.

00:10:23.998 --> 00:10:26.876
So it's, like, being in that role
where my team could lean on me.

00:10:26.959 --> 00:10:29.629
And just, like, just as far
as, like, the run game, blocking-wise,

00:10:29.837 --> 00:10:34.133
it's just so, something that's so new and foreign
to just my body type and my skill set.
- Yeah.

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:35.718
- So it's just improving in that

00:10:35.718 --> 00:10:36.719
and the little things,

00:10:36.719 --> 00:10:39.472
just the things that nobody really notices.
Like, the pass game

00:10:39.472 --> 00:10:42.767
I feel like I'm well on my way
and continuing to get better.

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:45.144
But it's just the run game
and those little things.

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:48.314
- How far are you from
10 Darren Waller?

00:10:48.522 --> 00:10:49.148
- You just continue

00:10:49.148 --> 00:10:52.401
to stack individual days at a time,
- Yeah.
- and you never know when that can happen.

00:10:52.401 --> 00:10:54.403
You know, I feel like
there will never be a time

00:10:54.403 --> 00:10:56.614
where I'm still playing football
and I can't improve at something.
- Mmhmm.

00:10:56.614 --> 00:10:58.991
- So I feel like I may
not even never get there.

00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:01.369
But, you know, it is what it is.

00:11:01.952 --> 00:11:06.082
- So when it comes to football,
what is driving you on the field?

00:11:06.082 --> 00:11:08.918
Like, are you a player that says, "OK,
I want to be the best tight end

00:11:09.126 --> 00:11:10.503
in the NFL right now"?

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:13.255
Is that a goal of yours,
or what is the thing

00:11:13.255 --> 00:11:16.467
that is pushing you to be better
when it comes to just playing football?

00:11:16.509 --> 00:11:19.345
- I wouldn't say my goal is to be
the best tight end in the NFL

00:11:19.345 --> 00:11:22.014
cuz I'm just not really
into comparing myself to other people.
- Mmhmm.

00:11:22.932 --> 00:11:26.102
- That just doesn't work
 well as far as my mental approach.

00:11:26.102 --> 00:11:28.646
Because I do that
and I'll start worrying about what

00:11:28.646 --> 00:11:30.648
other people are doing and worry
about stats, this and that.

00:11:30.648 --> 00:11:33.818
And it's, like, that
just drains my energy kind of.
- Mmhmm.

00:11:34.276 --> 00:11:38.197
- And so I just feel like me
giving the best effort I can and being the

00:11:38.197 --> 00:11:42.535
most prepared and having my attitude
in a positive manner at all times,

00:11:42.535 --> 00:11:44.370
that's what success is to me.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.539
And then all the results
will take care of themselves.

00:11:47.248 --> 00:11:49.667
- Mmhmm. So that's your goal every time you step onto the field.
- Yeah.

00:11:49.709 --> 00:11:51.711
- And that's how you approach the game.
- Right.

00:11:51.752 --> 00:11:55.005
- And you have seen that work for you?
- Yeah, it works for me.

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:58.008
It allows me to, you know, have an impact
on the people that are around me.
- Mmhmm.

00:11:58.134 --> 00:12:01.721
- And not just make it all about me
and it's my show or anything like that.
- Yeah.

00:12:01.721 --> 00:12:04.515
- It's about, you know, what kind of impact
I can have on the people around me.

00:12:04.515 --> 00:12:06.058
I try to be very conscious of that.

00:12:06.058 --> 00:12:08.394
- Yeah. I remember
I read this quote, and it said,

00:12:08.644 --> 00:12:11.147
"You should always do it out of love
 and never for it."

00:12:11.147 --> 00:12:14.734
- Yeah.
- Like, that's—
- I shared that exact quote.
- Did you?

00:12:15.025 --> 00:12:17.570
- On my Instagram probably a few weeks
 ago. So I know exactly what you're talking about.
- Oh, no way.

00:12:17.903 --> 00:12:21.115
You are very good quotes
on your Instagram stories.

00:12:21.115 --> 00:12:23.534
So if you aren't following
Darren on Instagram,

00:12:23.576 --> 00:12:25.286
you absolutely should
cuz there's always a good quote.

00:12:25.286 --> 00:12:28.664
You're sharing one, you're posting one.
- I'm sharing it for me, you know? Like...
- Yeah.

00:12:28.789 --> 00:12:31.584
- It's something that convicts me first,
and then I feel like it would

00:12:31.834 --> 00:12:34.545
convict somebody else.
- Yeah. No, and they definitely do.

00:12:34.587 --> 00:12:37.631
There's obviously so many things
that I wanna talk to you about.

00:12:38.007 --> 00:12:40.009
You are someone who it is
not an exaggeration to say

00:12:40.009 --> 00:12:44.180
is a complete open book,
which I think is important for people

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:47.933
to feel like they really understand
you and see what they see

00:12:47.933 --> 00:12:51.061
in themselves and you.
So I just applaud all that you do

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:53.606
in talking about your life
and what you've been through

00:12:53.606 --> 00:12:56.025
and where you're going.
I think it's really, really important.
- Thank you.

00:12:56.567 --> 00:13:01.280
- But something that really struck me
when I was watching a lot of interviews

00:13:01.822 --> 00:13:04.366
prepping for this one.
Obviously, a lot of people are talking

00:13:04.366 --> 00:13:07.703
about the same thing, which is your road
to recovery, your addiction.

00:13:08.204 --> 00:13:12.541
Is that something
that you enjoy discussing,

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:16.796
or is it a thing that you just continue
to be asked about, if that makes sense?

00:13:17.338 --> 00:13:18.923
- There's, I mean, there are some times

00:13:18.923 --> 00:13:21.634
where it's, like, I do
feel like it can get repetitive, but
- Mmhmm.

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:26.013
- at the same time, it keeps me in the mode
that I'm in when I'm talking about it.
- Yeah.

00:13:26.013 --> 00:13:30.893
- It makes me not, like, you know, forget
what life used to be like.
- Mmhmm.

00:13:31.894 --> 00:13:34.939
- And, you know, the kind
of thinking and the habits that I left behind.

00:13:34.939 --> 00:13:36.440
Because if I don't,

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.735
you know, do the work that's necessary day
in, day out, I could slip back into those.

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:43.405
And I do find myself, I sometimes,
like, my thinking, just kind of slipping

00:13:43.405 --> 00:13:46.075
if I'm just doing, doing, doing,
going, going, grinding, grinding

00:13:46.408 --> 00:13:50.913
and not focusing on myself like
- Yeah.
- I know that I should on a daily basis.

00:13:51.205 --> 00:13:52.373
You know, it can start to weigh on me, so.
- Yeah.

00:13:52.373 --> 00:13:55.167
- I just, talking about it keeps me,

00:13:55.209 --> 00:13:57.670
yeah, it just keeps me in that mode.
- Mmhmm.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.089
You said that sometimes
you'll find yourself going, going, going

00:14:00.589 --> 00:14:02.132
and thinking, OK, maybe you might slip.

00:14:02.132 --> 00:14:05.177
Can you tell me more about what
that looks like, what that feels like?

00:14:05.177 --> 00:14:08.931
- Yeah, it's just like I'm
so caught up in the performance of my

00:14:08.931 --> 00:14:12.142
day-to-day. Like, I'm in a performance
industry, you know, and just,

00:14:12.768 --> 00:14:16.647
my brand matters,
my stats matter, all these things matter.

00:14:16.647 --> 00:14:17.356
And it's just like...

00:14:18.315 --> 00:14:21.110
And I come across all these people,
and it's like they love me for what I do.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.487
But it's, like, when I take a step
back, it's like,

00:14:23.487 --> 00:14:25.322
what about, this is going to end someday.

00:14:25.322 --> 00:14:27.867
What is it going to be like then? Like,
who am I going to be? Like,

00:14:27.867 --> 00:14:31.161
am I going to be somebody that doesn't
know who I am when it's over with?

00:14:31.412 --> 00:14:32.997
And so it's, like, I gotta make sure

00:14:32.997 --> 00:14:35.583
that, you know, in the mornings
and afternoons, in the evenings, I'm,

00:14:36.166 --> 00:14:36.959
you know, just checking in

00:14:36.959 --> 00:14:40.045
and just being still and just listening
to what's going on inside me.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:43.883
- And yeah, it's just
gotta be a good balance.

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:47.177
Like, even this last week,
like, I'm feeling kind of overwhelmed.

00:14:47.177 --> 00:14:49.680
I'm, like, I'm going to these events,
and all these people want to take pictures,

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:52.266
and it's just, like,
all right, like, this is cool.

00:14:52.266 --> 00:14:53.976
Like, I understand what it means to them.

00:14:53.976 --> 00:14:55.352
But it's, like, it's

00:14:55.352 --> 00:14:57.771
not like they really try
to get to know me, and so it's like

00:14:57.813 --> 00:15:01.025
I'm spending all my time doing this,
and I imagine that amount of time with,

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:04.820
you know, journaling or meditating
or talking to people in my life that

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:06.780
I could really talk about
real-life shit with.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:09.992
- Yeah.
- I don't know if you can cuss.
- You can cuss. Cuss as much as you want.
- All right.
- Yeah.

00:15:10.242 --> 00:15:13.078
So when you were checking in
with yourself, what are the questions

00:15:13.078 --> 00:15:15.164
you're asking yourself?
- Like, who are you?

00:15:15.164 --> 00:15:18.292
It's just like, to see
if I'm, if you know, my ego

00:15:18.292 --> 00:15:22.796
wants to attach to football
or if it wants to attach to music

00:15:22.796 --> 00:15:25.925
or if it wants to attach
to any of these other things or instead of, like,

00:15:26.216 --> 00:15:29.553
you know, just my values.
Like, making sure that I'm staying on those

00:15:29.553 --> 00:15:32.181
and then just listening.
Cuz thoughts are gonna come up.

00:15:32.598 --> 00:15:36.727
And then that's where I can kinda sift
through 'em and realize, like, you know,

00:15:36.727 --> 00:15:39.855
I don't have to really go with the narrative
that my mind wants to do

00:15:39.855 --> 00:15:42.524
because it's always
just chirping and chirping and
chirping and just

00:15:43.442 --> 00:15:45.736
trying to beat me up one minute
and then trying to make me think

00:15:45.736 --> 00:15:47.363
I'm the greatest thing
in the world the next minute.

00:15:47.363 --> 00:15:50.616
So it's just trying to, like, find
that even ground.
- Yeah.

00:15:50.658 --> 00:15:54.328
And that's what you're trying
to stay on track with every single day.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:55.037
- Yeah.
- So when you talk about your journey
and things that have happened to you,

00:15:55.037 --> 00:15:58.499
- Yeah.
- So when you talk about your journey
and things that have happened to you,

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:00.834
does it make you sad?

00:16:01.085 --> 00:16:02.836
Does it just make you reflective?

00:16:02.836 --> 00:16:06.173
Does it make you proud of where
you've come? Like, what emotions come

00:16:06.173 --> 00:16:10.010
with discussing it but then also, like,
discussing it all the time?

00:16:10.177 --> 00:16:12.137
- There's a lot of times
where I can kind of, like, laugh

00:16:12.137 --> 00:16:14.098
looking back in a way,
and people will kind of be, like,

00:16:14.098 --> 00:16:16.558
how can you even laugh
about that kind of stuff?
- Yeah.

00:16:16.558 --> 00:16:17.810
- But it's like, you know, you

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:21.105
do the work to kind of move on from it
and see it in a different way.

00:16:21.146 --> 00:16:25.359
But I do kinda get sad
because there are some times where

00:16:26.485 --> 00:16:27.653
I'll still think, like, I don't

00:16:27.653 --> 00:16:30.906
deserve good things,
even though I may be in this space.

00:16:30.906 --> 00:16:31.991
And there's, like,
a lot of good has happened.

00:16:31.991 --> 00:16:35.995
I'm still so accustomed to thinking in
that mode where things weren't going well
- Mmhmm.

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:40.499
- and how, like, just that place of, you know, suffering
and just hopelessness

00:16:40.499 --> 00:16:41.291
kinda got comfortable.

00:16:41.291 --> 00:16:43.794
Like, I was telling you about the person
that was underground.

00:16:44.128 --> 00:16:45.921
- Yeah.
- And it's just you get
kinda stuck in that mode.

00:16:45.921 --> 00:16:46.964
And now things are going well.

00:16:46.964 --> 00:16:49.466
It's like, there are days
when I can find a way

00:16:49.466 --> 00:16:51.218
to just be like,
"How do I just blow this shit up?"

00:16:51.218 --> 00:16:54.263
And just like,
- Yeah.
- because some days I just not really be feeling it.

00:16:54.638 --> 00:16:57.141
But to bring myself out of it

00:16:57.266 --> 00:17:00.185
into the positive, like, that's
what talking about it does for me.

00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:02.521
You just put it in the air,
and it's kinda like, all right,

00:17:02.521 --> 00:17:05.274
like, it's not as bad as I'm making it
seem, you know? My mind

00:17:05.274 --> 00:17:07.776
can make me think I'm alone
sometimes.
- Yeah.
- It's, like, I'm not alone.

00:17:07.776 --> 00:17:09.528
Like, people are here for me. They love me.
They support me.

00:17:09.528 --> 00:17:12.197
So I gotta just keep talking,

00:17:12.197 --> 00:17:13.907
keep that dialogue going.
- Yeah.

00:17:13.907 --> 00:17:16.493
So you feel like in
some ways there was, like,

00:17:16.493 --> 00:17:19.496
kind of a home in the sorrow?
Like…
- Yeah.
- Yeah?

00:17:19.788 --> 00:17:21.832
- It's, like, I just got, like I said,
I got comfortable there,

00:17:22.082 --> 00:17:25.252
and there was times where it was like,
yeah, I wanted to change,

00:17:25.669 --> 00:17:30.174
and I would say like, "Yeah, I'm
about to stop doing these pills on Friday."

00:17:30.174 --> 00:17:33.177
And Friday would come, and I'd be like,
"Mm, nah, I'm going to go through the weekend."
- Mmhmm.

00:17:33.469 --> 00:17:35.846
- And it's just like,
you know, you get so comfortable, and it's,

00:17:35.846 --> 00:17:39.266
like, I don't, that change seems way
harder than just sitting

00:17:39.266 --> 00:17:40.934
right here in it does.

00:17:40.934 --> 00:17:43.187
- Mmm. Can you dive into that more?

00:17:43.187 --> 00:17:44.396
Cuz I think that's interesting.

00:17:44.396 --> 00:17:47.775
- Yeah. I mean, you just get so used to,
you become a victim,

00:17:47.775 --> 00:17:51.570
and you start making excuses
and justifying why you do

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:54.990
certain things.
- Yeah.
- Or justifying why you judge people in a certain way.

00:17:55.324 --> 00:17:57.576
And it's like, you know,
you get comfortable there.

00:17:57.576 --> 00:17:58.827
And then when it's, like,
when it's time for you

00:17:58.827 --> 00:18:02.539
to look at your own problems, you know,
clean up your own side of the street,

00:18:02.998 --> 00:18:04.458
that's some dirty business.

00:18:04.458 --> 00:18:06.835
That's like, that can get pretty
nasty and ugly.
- Yeah.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:10.130
- And not a lot of people
wanna look in that mirror, you know?
- Mmhmm.

00:18:10.672 --> 00:18:12.508
- So it's easier to just

00:18:12.508 --> 00:18:14.843
be where you at
and be in the habits that you created.

00:18:15.469 --> 00:18:17.930
- So you said that, you know,
sometimes you struggle with, saying,

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:21.391
"OK, do I deserve good things?" Or, like,
 "Do I just wanna blow this shit up?"

00:18:21.391 --> 00:18:24.895
Can you give me an example of a time
that you have felt that way?

00:18:25.229 --> 00:18:27.940
- I mean, keeping it real,
there was, like, a few days ago where I woke

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:28.732
up, and I was like, "Man, like,

00:18:31.193 --> 00:18:33.862
I'm here, and, like, I'm here
for a reason. God has me

00:18:33.862 --> 00:18:36.865
here playing football for a reason."
But today I'm not feeling this shit, like.

00:18:36.907 --> 00:18:39.535
- Yeah.
 - Like, fuck this today, like for real.
- Mmhmm.

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:45.040
- And it's like, but that's when I can kind of
sit down and be still and just, you know,

00:18:45.582 --> 00:18:49.586
listen to myself and listen to who God is to me.

00:18:49.878 --> 00:18:52.589
And that's when I can come
back down and be, like,

00:18:52.589 --> 00:18:54.883
all right, like, you know,
you're a little over there.

00:18:54.925 --> 00:18:57.803
You can just come back over here to the middle
a little bit and relax, like,

00:18:58.178 --> 00:18:59.388
this day is gonna get better.

00:18:59.388 --> 00:19:01.640
We haven't even got started yet,
and you all the way down there.
- [Laughing]

00:19:01.932 --> 00:19:04.810
- So just come on back here, and...
- Bring it back. Yeah.
- We can figure this thing out.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:07.604
- So someone is going to hear
that and say, wow,

00:19:07.604 --> 00:19:12.651
so a few days ago, an NFL player felt like,
"I just wanna blow this shit up.

00:19:12.901 --> 00:19:15.028
This isn't what I wanna do right now."

00:19:15.028 --> 00:19:17.531
What is your response, then, in terms of,

00:19:17.573 --> 00:19:21.326
right, like, just because I do this
and you see me doing this and

00:19:21.326 --> 00:19:25.289
I can catch a football and I can make a
touchdown, do all these big plays,

00:19:25.289 --> 00:19:29.293
there are still times where I'm struggling
with so many things
- Yeah.
- that you're

00:19:29.293 --> 00:19:30.419
still not seeing?
- Right.

00:19:30.419 --> 00:19:31.670
- Cuz you mean,
you're like, a few days ago,

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:35.966
it's like, well dang, a few days ago
there was a game, like, y'all played good,

00:19:35.966 --> 00:19:39.303
like, what is it about that that still can
bring you back to certain places?

00:19:39.469 --> 00:19:41.680
- I mean, it's just, like, it's challenging.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:43.557
And, you know, I used to just

00:19:43.557 --> 00:19:47.728
run from challenges, whether it was, like,
a tough conversation or, you know,

00:19:48.145 --> 00:19:50.814
just having to own up to my stuff
or having to get my act together.

00:19:50.814 --> 00:19:52.274
Like, I would always just,

00:19:52.274 --> 00:19:54.651
you know, run from it,
whether it be in the forms of drugs and

00:19:54.693 --> 00:19:58.238
alcohol or women or anything.
Like, you can run from things

00:19:58.238 --> 00:19:59.489
in a lot of different ways.

00:19:59.489 --> 00:20:02.034
And so that would just be my habit.

00:20:02.034 --> 00:20:05.746
And now it's, like, nah, like, my character
now is to just face those things head-on.

00:20:06.079 --> 00:20:06.872
And that can be tough.

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:08.332
It's, like, I'm not waking up everyday, like,

00:20:08.332 --> 00:20:10.500
I'm going right towards
my challenges.
- Yeah [Laughing]
- I'm just gonna, you know,

00:20:11.585 --> 00:20:13.170
- Let's conquer!
- This is going to be great today.

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:16.423
You know, most of the time it's like, man,
I really gotta have this conversation.

00:20:16.423 --> 00:20:19.635
Or I really need to sit here
and reflect on this or really

00:20:19.635 --> 00:20:23.347
find something that I can take
and grow forward with.

00:20:23.555 --> 00:20:27.601
And so, yeah, it's not always—like,
football is a challenging game,

00:20:28.227 --> 00:20:31.104
just putting up with all the marketing
and dealing with all these people.

00:20:31.104 --> 00:20:33.482
And it's like, it's a challenging thing
for somebody like me

00:20:33.482 --> 00:20:36.485
that wants to isolate as well. So
it's, in a lot of challenging environments

00:20:36.902 --> 00:20:39.988
and just telling myself, like,
this challenge is gonna help me grow.

00:20:39.988 --> 00:20:42.157
It's not gonna just tear me apart

00:20:42.157 --> 00:20:43.742
or anything. Like, it's here
for me to learn lessons,

00:20:43.742 --> 00:20:46.078
and I go on to the next one
and just keep going.

00:20:46.078 --> 00:20:50.999
- Mmhmm.
I know you were first introduced
when you were 15...
- Yeah.
- years old.

00:20:51.541 --> 00:20:53.752
- Can you talk to me about that moment,

00:20:54.211 --> 00:20:56.463
what drug it was you tried,

00:20:56.463 --> 00:20:59.258
how it happened?
Just tell me the story of that day.

00:20:59.341 --> 00:21:02.094
- Yeah, so, leading up to that moment,
you know, I was always like,

00:21:02.135 --> 00:21:04.721
you know, trying to be, like,
almost a goody two-shoes kid.

00:21:04.721 --> 00:21:06.390
Like, I was like I'm not gonna drink,

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:09.059
not gonna smoke. Like, kind of seeing it
from a place of self-righteousness.
- Mm.

00:21:09.518 --> 00:21:14.189
- But then, you know, I guess I was, like, 15,
and I was good at football growing up.

00:21:14.189 --> 00:21:17.192
But then people started growing past me,
and I was real small,
- Yeah.

00:21:17.192 --> 00:21:20.362
- like my first years of high school,
and so I was riding the bench.
- Yeah.

00:21:20.904 --> 00:21:23.782
- And so I kinda put my eggs into the football basket
pretty early on.

00:21:23.782 --> 00:21:25.534
And then it was just like, well,
that's not working.

00:21:25.534 --> 00:21:27.869
And I wasn't, like, popular
or anything like that.

00:21:27.869 --> 00:21:30.414
So I was just kinda, like, lost and feeling,
you know, kinda sad.

00:21:30.747 --> 00:21:33.417
And, you know, my friends presented it
in a way that was, like, this will

00:21:33.417 --> 00:21:35.961
make you feel good. And I was like,
you know, I'd like to feel good,

00:21:35.961 --> 00:21:39.339
so.
- Yeah.
- You know, I'll try it, like, I got nothing
to lose right now.

00:21:39.631 --> 00:21:42.926
And so I tried it,
and it was just, like, this works.

00:21:43.051 --> 00:21:45.137
Like, it's like, it makes me feel good.

00:21:45.178 --> 00:21:47.222
Like, it give me the tingles and all that.

00:21:47.222 --> 00:21:51.143
And then it's also, like, my mind's always
revving at a thousand miles per hour.

00:21:51.143 --> 00:21:53.562
It slows my mind down.
- Mmhmm.
- And it's like, OK, like,

00:21:54.604 --> 00:21:56.940
these people, like,
do the same thing I'm doing.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:58.567
It allowed me to make friends.

00:21:58.567 --> 00:22:01.069
So it was, like, all those things made it, like,
oh, yeah, this is just,

00:22:01.236 --> 00:22:03.405
this is what I do now. This is who I am.
- Mmhmm.

00:22:03.905 --> 00:22:06.742
- And never even thought

00:22:06.742 --> 00:22:10.037
of giving it up for the next
10 years.
- Yeah.

00:22:10.787 --> 00:22:14.458
Do people underestimate, like,
how hard it is to fall into drugs?

00:22:14.875 --> 00:22:16.877
- Yeah. I mean, it's real easy.
- Or how easy it is to fall into drugs?

00:22:16.877 --> 00:22:20.047
- Yeah, I would say, yeah, it's easy.
And, like, looking back, it was like,

00:22:20.213 --> 00:22:24.009
people was like, "Weed is a gateway drug." People were
like, "Man, weed ain't no gateway drug."

00:22:24.009 --> 00:22:27.095
I was, like, for me,
I mean, I see for a lot of people

00:22:27.095 --> 00:22:30.515
how it is, but, like, for me,
like I didn't even start with weed.

00:22:30.515 --> 00:22:33.060
So it was just, like,
a whole different experience.

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:35.395
People were like, "Damn, like,
you just started with pills?" And I was just like,

00:22:35.437 --> 00:22:39.066
I mean, yeah, I didn't, this is just where
I ended up.
- Yeah.

00:22:39.358 --> 00:22:42.903
And so what specifically, you said
you started with pills? What...
- It was, like, Percocets.

00:22:43.111 --> 00:22:45.989
The, you know, pain pills
you get from a surgery, Oxycodone,

00:22:46.031 --> 00:22:47.866
hydros, stuff like that.
- Yeah.

00:22:47.866 --> 00:22:49.034
Started drinking after that, and

00:22:49.034 --> 00:22:52.037
then I started smoking weed. Then Xanax,

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:56.666
Adderall, cocaine, molly.

00:22:57.793 --> 00:23:01.129
Probably like that. Never did,
I always say, like, I never got into heroin,

00:23:01.129 --> 00:23:04.549
but I was, like, I mean, Percocets
are basically heroin in a different form,

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:07.844
like.
- Mmhmm.
- So I'm not better than nobody else cuz

00:23:07.969 --> 00:23:11.181
I didn't
- Yeah.
- try heroin, you know, so it was just damn near everything.

00:23:11.848 --> 00:23:16.520
- One moment you talk about was when you
were in your car and you overdosed.
- Yeah.

00:23:16.728 --> 00:23:18.480
I believe that was what, 2017?

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:19.731
- Oh, yeah, yup. August 11, 2017.

00:23:19.731 --> 00:23:22.317
- Yeah. August 11, 2017.

00:23:23.151 --> 00:23:27.531
In that moment when you overdosed,
did you think, like, that was it?

00:23:27.531 --> 00:23:30.200
Like, was that your thought?

00:23:30.367 --> 00:23:33.954
- I did not think it was it,
but I was like, I'm about to, like,

00:23:33.954 --> 00:23:35.163
pass out or something, like.

00:23:35.163 --> 00:23:37.958
- Yeah.
- Cuz I was, like I said,
I was at the grocery store parking lot,

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:42.879
and I was going to, like, get some beer or something,
and I was getting outta the

00:23:43.004 --> 00:23:44.756
car, and it was just like,

00:23:44.756 --> 00:23:47.926
nah, I can't get out of the car.
I feel like I'm just...fall out on the ground,

00:23:47.926 --> 00:23:49.344
and it's, like, midday.

00:23:49.344 --> 00:23:52.305
So I was like, nah,
let me just sit in the car and just chill.
- Mmhmm.
- And, uh...

00:23:53.306 --> 00:23:55.475
And I was high as shit. Like, I
didn't have the car on.

00:23:55.475 --> 00:23:56.768
Like, I was just sitting there.
- Yeah.

00:23:56.768 --> 00:24:01.606
And just...it was like somebody pulled the TV out
from, the cord from behind the TV

00:24:01.606 --> 00:24:04.526
and just the TV just [powering down sound effect].
And it was just like, just went black.

00:24:04.651 --> 00:24:08.071
I feel like I woke up like a second later,
and it was, like, nighttime.

00:24:08.405 --> 00:24:11.241
And I was, like, the sweatiest
I've ever been in my life.
- Mmhmm.

00:24:11.241 --> 00:24:14.911
- And it was cold and, like,
just like off, and

00:24:16.037 --> 00:24:19.332
it was, like...I was, like, scared. It
scared me enough, I was like, yeah, like this

00:24:19.332 --> 00:24:21.168
the first time I feel like
I'm not in control.

00:24:21.168 --> 00:24:24.921
Like, I always felt like I was in control,
like I was, like, master manipulator.

00:24:24.921 --> 00:24:27.382
- Mmhmm.
- Like, I could craft words in a way

00:24:27.382 --> 00:24:31.428
to sell you a dream and tell you
I wasn't doing something that I was doing.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:33.722
And just, like, a finesser, you know?
- Yeah.

00:24:33.722 --> 00:24:36.475
- So that's just who I was, and at that point,

00:24:36.475 --> 00:24:38.602
I could no longer, like, I couldn't fool myself.

00:24:38.643 --> 00:24:41.188
I could fool everybody else.
But I couldn't fool myself.
- Mmhmm.

00:24:41.354 --> 00:24:44.608
- So that was scary enough
for me to not wanna do it ever again.

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:47.360
- Yeah, it was like that
"looking in the mirror" moment.
- Yeah.

00:24:47.736 --> 00:24:49.654
- Like, "All right, this is something
that I'm gonna have to change.

00:24:49.654 --> 00:24:51.114
And I have decided."
- Right.

00:24:51.114 --> 00:24:53.408
- I think you said in an interview
that it was Fentanyl?

00:24:54.326 --> 00:24:56.745
- Yeah, it had to be, cuz
I was picking up the same pills.

00:24:56.745 --> 00:25:01.208
Like, it wasn't, like, the most absurd
amount of pills ever, but it was, like, what

00:25:01.208 --> 00:25:04.628
I had usually been getting,
and it would have me, like,

00:25:05.545 --> 00:25:08.590
just on the couch
googly-eyed and shit. But, like,

00:25:09.591 --> 00:25:11.551
it was something off about that
batch, you know? That

00:25:11.551 --> 00:25:16.056
shit just put me down like "bam,"
so I knew there was something in it.

00:25:16.431 --> 00:25:19.100
- So that has been in the
news a lot recently. Fentanyl use.

00:25:19.434 --> 00:25:22.395
- Yeah, there's a girl from my third
grade class overdosed and died last night.

00:25:23.146 --> 00:25:27.567
- No way.
- There was a girl I used to have a crush
on in my third grade class.
- Oh, no.
- Yeah.

00:25:27.567 --> 00:25:31.238
Me and some of my friends,
we had dinner with her, like, back in

00:25:31.696 --> 00:25:34.115
February. It was around,
like, the All-Star weekend.
- You just saw her.
- Yeah.

00:25:34.533 --> 00:25:36.535
And yeah, she died last night.

00:25:36.535 --> 00:25:39.287
- So when you see news,
not just about someone,

00:25:39.287 --> 00:25:43.792
you know, specifically just news about,
like, how deadly Fentanyl is

00:25:43.792 --> 00:25:47.003
and how it's affecting people,
is that a moment that is sad for you?

00:25:47.003 --> 00:25:48.171
Is it triggering for you?

00:25:48.171 --> 00:25:51.132
What is it like when you hear
about Fentanyl in the news

00:25:51.132 --> 00:25:54.177
cuz it's happening so much?
- Yeah, it's just depressing, you know?
- Yeah.

00:25:54.302 --> 00:25:57.847
- It doesn't really, like, trigger me
or anything, but it's like, it's just like,

00:25:58.431 --> 00:26:03.019
it's depressing seeing, like, how people think
that these substances

00:26:03.395 --> 00:26:06.982
can in a way, like, cure their problems
or, like, make these things go away.

00:26:07.148 --> 00:26:09.651
It was like, you know, I realized
like every time I got high, it was like

00:26:10.193 --> 00:26:11.987
my problems went away
for, like, a little bit.

00:26:11.987 --> 00:26:16.324
But as soon as I woke up the next morning
or as soon as I came down, it was right outside the door

00:26:16.741 --> 00:26:19.578
waiting for me like, "Come on."
- Yeah.
- Like, "You gonna have to deal with me at some point."

00:26:19.744 --> 00:26:21.580
- Yeah.
- So it's just sad that people think, like,

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:26.626
that's a way out or an escape
when it's really, like, you just delaying,

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:29.212
you know, what you're gonna
have to face at some point.

00:26:29.963 --> 00:26:33.800
So I was listening to your second album,
"Delusions of Clarity,"

00:26:34.217 --> 00:26:37.137
and there was—
- Third, technically.
- Third, technically. My bad.

00:26:37.554 --> 00:26:39.014
And there was a lyric in there—

00:26:39.014 --> 00:26:41.433
I wrote it down, let me
make sure I say it right—

00:26:41.433 --> 00:26:43.977
where you talk about depression
controlling your mind.

00:26:44.185 --> 00:26:45.854
- Yeah.
- I think that was in the intro song.

00:26:45.854 --> 00:26:50.775
- Uh-huh.
- Were you saying that in a hyperbolic
way writing the lyric,

00:26:50.775 --> 00:26:53.278
or is depression something that you do
genuinely struggle with?

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:55.822
- Um, yeah, it's something
that I struggle with for sure.

00:26:55.905 --> 00:26:57.866
- Yeah.
- You know, it doesn't control my mind like

00:26:57.866 --> 00:27:02.746
it used to, but it can kinda, you know,
dictate my days for a little bit.

00:27:02.787 --> 00:27:06.166
Like, it won't be like a stretch of weeks
or months at a time.

00:27:06.166 --> 00:27:09.919
But it could be, like, a day where I'm
just kinda like, man,

00:27:10.128 --> 00:27:12.881
you know? People may be like, "Yo,
like, what's up? You good?" Cuz

00:27:12.964 --> 00:27:16.760
usually I'll be, like, roasting people
or, like, you know, just being a goofball.

00:27:16.760 --> 00:27:20.597
But then it's like some days, I just be like,
you could just tell I'm not really with it,

00:27:20.597 --> 00:27:23.350
you know? I'll do what I have to do,
but I'm not really like,

00:27:24.017 --> 00:27:26.978
you know, in my most bubbly
for my personality.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:29.898
- Mmhmm.
- And so it, it does, it does still show up.

00:27:30.315 --> 00:27:32.859
And, uh, it's like the other part of that
lyric is like, "Depression control my mind.

00:27:32.859 --> 00:27:33.985
You wouldn't even know
cuz I'm grinning."

00:27:33.985 --> 00:27:37.614
- Yeah.
- It's like, you know, like I said,
I can always manipulate and also, like,

00:27:37.656 --> 00:27:41.117
laugh it off and put the smile on
and tell a joke and like,

00:27:41.743 --> 00:27:42.994
you know, be in that for the moment.

00:27:42.994 --> 00:27:45.121
But then it's like,
you know, when I'm by myself,

00:27:45.121 --> 00:27:48.041
it's just, like, I'm back in that hole a little bit.
- Yeah.

00:27:48.291 --> 00:27:51.086
So when I think about you, right, and

00:27:51.086 --> 00:27:54.506
everything that you have gone through,
and then I think about a place like Vegas—

00:27:55.131 --> 00:27:58.176
- Right. It sound crazy on paper.

00:27:58.218 --> 00:27:59.969
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:27:59.969 --> 00:28:02.722
- How—I mean, what
went through your mind, right?

00:28:02.722 --> 00:28:05.767
Like, "I'm going to be in Vegas."

00:28:06.351 --> 00:28:09.312
Like, is that something you're like,
"All right, this could not be great"?

00:28:09.562 --> 00:28:11.981
Like, are those thoughts
that you have in your head sometimes?

00:28:12.107 --> 00:28:14.359
- I mean, that's just where I think
God is just hilarious

00:28:14.359 --> 00:28:18.655
