but is there, like, an element of

00:43:12.923 --> 00:43:14.216
almost, like, guilt

00:43:14.216 --> 00:43:18.345
when someone that you know is in a place
that you once were but you no longer are in?

00:43:19.597 --> 00:43:21.765
- Yeah. It's like—yeah. Cuz, you

00:43:21.765 --> 00:43:24.977
know, it's like I have the—
you know, I've been through it.

00:43:25.019 --> 00:43:28.564
You know, I kinda have an idea
of what it takes to, you know,

00:43:29.064 --> 00:43:30.441
see things on the other side.

00:43:30.441 --> 00:43:32.568
But it's like, and then I think that

00:43:33.068 --> 00:43:35.654
my ego thinks that
that directly applies to me going

00:43:35.654 --> 00:43:38.699
and taking that person and walking them
through everything and making them—

00:43:38.699 --> 00:43:41.035
but it's like, nah, they have to have
their own experience.
- Mmhmm.

00:43:41.035 --> 00:43:44.830
- So it's like I have to view that voice
as like, nah, that's irrational.

00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:46.999
I'm not attaching myself to that.

00:43:46.999 --> 00:43:49.877
And I'm not gonna allow that to be
what's going on in my head.
- Mmhmm.

00:43:49.877 --> 00:43:53.672
- So it's just, it's
a battle, day in, at all times.

00:43:53.964 --> 00:43:56.091
- Well, I mean, you have affected
so many people, but you know,

00:43:56.091 --> 00:43:58.802
you can always think, "I know
I directly affected Maxx."

00:43:59.428 --> 00:44:02.139
So, you know, there is, like, a huge impact
that you are having on

00:44:02.139 --> 00:44:05.476
so many players,
people, whoever, just human beings, so.

00:44:05.476 --> 00:44:07.978
- Right.
- Just keep that with you. Your peak?

00:44:09.063 --> 00:44:13.275
- Hmm. My peak. I would say

00:44:14.276 --> 00:44:16.737
it was probably, like, a month ago.

00:44:17.529 --> 00:44:21.575
My foundation, we started giving grants

00:44:21.575 --> 00:44:26.163
to people to go to treatment
at this place called Landmark Recovery.

00:44:26.163 --> 00:44:27.915
It's, like, North Vegas,
not too far from here.

00:44:27.915 --> 00:44:30.459
- Mmhmm.
- And, you know,

00:44:31.377 --> 00:44:35.673
they give you, like, the grant applications
of, you know, people like age 18 to 26

00:44:35.673 --> 00:44:39.510
that don't really have the money to pay
for it themselves

00:44:39.927 --> 00:44:42.054
and, or the insurance or anything.

00:44:42.054 --> 00:44:45.057
And so it's like, I approved the grants.
I see the names on there.

00:44:45.474 --> 00:44:50.521
And, you know, it's Heather, Greg and Desmond.
And you see the names, like, OK, like, this,

00:44:51.188 --> 00:44:52.272
I feel like they could benefit from this,

00:44:52.272 --> 00:44:54.108
from reading, like, their profile
and their story and everything.

00:44:54.108 --> 00:44:57.236
And they go through the program,
but then it's like, I go to the place,

00:44:57.236 --> 00:45:00.864
and I actually got to meet them.
- Oh, great.

00:45:01.782 --> 00:45:03.951
- And they, like, were

00:45:03.951 --> 00:45:09.915
just, like, oozing with gratitude.
- Yeah.
- And just hearing their stories. Like,

00:45:10.666 --> 00:45:16.922
you know, Heather was
in and out of prison and has a child

00:45:17.464 --> 00:45:19.633
and is, like, you know,
trying to turn her life around.

00:45:19.633 --> 00:45:23.303
And she was like, "This experience is
what is allowing me to do that."

00:45:23.345 --> 00:45:27.433
- Yeah.
- And there was this dude Desmond
who was, he said he was just, like, walking out

00:45:27.433 --> 00:45:31.353
in the desert, was just like,
said he was just over this shit. And was like,

00:45:32.438 --> 00:45:33.856
you know, thought he was gonna die out there

00:45:33.856 --> 00:45:37.151
cuz he was walking around with, like, no water,
not eating nothing.
- Mmhmm.

00:45:38.235 --> 00:45:40.779
- And he was like, "This being here
and this program

00:45:41.321 --> 00:45:45.117
has changed me and, like, you know,
I have a new outlook on life, new hope."

00:45:45.117 --> 00:45:47.369
And it's just like being around them
and just talking to them,

00:45:47.369 --> 00:45:50.873
it was crazy. And it was like,
you know, the foundation,

00:45:50.873 --> 00:45:52.750
they try to, like, get the word out to people,

00:45:52.750 --> 00:45:54.585
so there was, like, cameras
and news people there.
- Yeah.

00:45:54.585 --> 00:45:57.337
- And so I thought it was just gonna be,
like, a five-minute thing.

00:45:57.379 --> 00:45:59.298
I kinda, like, shake their hand
and make it look cute.

00:45:59.298 --> 00:46:00.966
But it was like, we were sitting there talking for, like,

00:46:00.966 --> 00:46:02.718
45 minutes, and the news people were just like—
- That's great.

00:46:03.719 --> 00:46:06.805
- Like, what is—and it was just
awesome to connect with them.

00:46:07.014 --> 00:46:08.766
- Yeah.
- And realize, like, those moments

00:46:08.766 --> 00:46:11.602
where it's like, I can be in my head
and be like, "Am I really impacting people?"

00:46:11.602 --> 00:46:14.980
But they were there like, "Bro, thank you."
- Living. Breathing.

00:46:15.105 --> 00:46:18.442
- Like, "thank you." And I was just like,
wow, this is a bit crazier

00:46:18.525 --> 00:46:21.195
than anything on a football field
or anything like that.

00:46:21.403 --> 00:46:24.114
- Oh, I love that. Well, you were a pleasure.

00:46:24.406 --> 00:46:28.327
You are easily one of my favorite people
that I have interviewed.

00:46:28.786 --> 00:46:31.622
And not because I think you're perfect,
but because, like, I know you're not.

00:46:31.622 --> 00:46:34.041
Because you tell us,
because you talk about everything

00:46:34.041 --> 00:46:35.501
that you've been through
and where you're going.

00:46:35.501 --> 00:46:38.003
So I deeply appreciate the time.

00:46:38.003 --> 00:46:41.507
But you gotta end with your favorite
quote since you're, like, quote master.

00:46:41.507 --> 00:46:44.468
And I love quotes. Like, I have quotes
in this show all the time.

00:46:44.468 --> 00:46:46.053
So tell me your favorite.
- It's tough, man.

00:46:47.137 --> 00:46:51.016
The one I go with is Winston Churchill.
Shoutout to Winston Churchill.

00:46:51.016 --> 00:46:52.059
- Shoutout to Winston.

00:46:52.059 --> 00:46:55.729
- That guy's tatted on my ankle,
- Shoutout Winston Churchill.

00:46:56.522 --> 00:46:59.608
- For real.
- I've never heard anyone say that.
- [Laughing]

00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:04.905
Yeah, nah, but, he said, "Success is moving from failure
to failure without losing enthusiasm."

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:08.534
- Mmm. Shoutout to Winston.
- That shit hit different.

00:47:09.117 --> 00:47:11.245
- Yeah. What do you take from that?

00:47:11.370 --> 00:47:14.581
- For me, it's, like, success
isn't always in the results.

00:47:14.748 --> 00:47:19.002
But more so, like, just how I show up
every day. Like, my process,

00:47:19.253 --> 00:47:22.965
you know? The impact that I leave
is not about, you know,

00:47:23.340 --> 00:47:26.760
anything that's in a bank account—
- Yeah.
- Or in the driveway or anything like that.

00:47:26.760 --> 00:47:32.099
It's just, you know, I can fail
and fall on my face in a thousand times,

00:47:32.099 --> 00:47:36.353
but I could still live a life
that is impactful and has meaning,

00:47:36.353 --> 00:47:40.524
you know? It doesn't have to be perfect.
It doesn't have to fit an image or be like,

00:47:40.524 --> 00:47:41.525
you know, "Everything's great over here."

00:47:41.525 --> 00:47:46.113
Like, shit can suck.
- Yeah.
- But I can still, you know, consider myself successful

00:47:46.113 --> 00:47:49.283
because I'm putting forth the effort
and trying to live life

00:47:49.283 --> 00:47:51.243
with the right attitude
and just the right perspective.

00:47:51.243 --> 00:47:53.161
- Yes. And success is what you make it.

